export const books = [{"id": "frankenstein", "title": "Frankenstein", "author": "Mary Shelley", "preface": ["Frankenstein is unusually interested in what happens to an ambition after it has been achieved. Victor imagines the glory of creating life with far more energy than he imagines the needs of the life he might create. The discovery promises to make him exceptional. Responsibility would require a quieter, more sustained form of attention.", "Mary Shelley gives the being at the centre of this ambition a voice, an education, and a desire to belong. The creature learns about human life from its edges, watching the affection and ordinary cooperation from which he is excluded. Language offers access to a world whose inhabitants may still refuse him recognition. Understanding other people does not guarantee that they will consent to understand him.", "The novel's sympathy is powerful precisely because it does not settle every judgment. Suffering can explain a grievance without granting unlimited rights over others. Victor and the creature both tell persuasive stories about what has been done to them, and each can make his own responsibility harder to see. Their accounts arrive through other listeners, so the act of being moved by a story becomes part of the book's concern.", "Its landscapes widen this human loneliness. Mountains, ice, and great distances offer beauty and apparent escape, but they do not remove the need for another person's response. The desire to venture beyond ordinary limits can be exhilarating and can leave ordinary obligations terribly neglected.", "The book has become a shorthand for the dangers of invention, though its questions reach well beyond a laboratory. Creating something establishes a relationship that admiration for the achievement cannot fulfil. Shelley asks what is owed to a being whose existence has become inconvenient, and what happens when the wish to be extraordinary outruns the capacity to care."], "characters": [{"id": "victor", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "central", "aliases": ["Victor Frankenstein", "Victor"], "subtitle": "A young Genevan natural philosopher", "body": "The narrator of the central story, obsessed since boyhood with the secrets of life and death. He leaves his family in Geneva to study at Ingolstadt, where his research takes a direction he will not fully explain even to the friend he is writing to."}, {"id": "creature", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "central", "aliases": ["the creature", "the daemon", "the monster", "the wretch", "the fiend"], "subtitle": "The being Victor makes", "body": "Assembled by Victor from collected remains and given life in his workshop. He is abandoned by his creator within moments of opening his eyes, before either of them has any idea what he is capable of."}, {"id": "walton", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "major", "aliases": ["Robert Walton", "Walton"], "subtitle": "An English captain bound for the North Pole", "body": "Writing letters home to his sister as his ship pushes into arctic ice, he pulls a half-frozen stranger aboard and becomes the outer frame through which the whole account reaches the reader. His own hunger for glory mirrors the story he is about to hear."}, {"id": "margaret-saville", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "reference", "aliases": ["Mrs. Saville", "Margaret"], "subtitle": "Walton's sister in England", "body": "The unseen addressee of Walton's letters, which open and close the novel. She never appears directly; everything the reader learns of her comes through her brother's writing."}, {"id": "alphonse-frankenstein", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["Alphonse Frankenstein", "M. Frankenstein"], "subtitle": "Victor's father", "body": "A respected Genevan syndic who raised his children with affection and encouraged Victor's education without grasping where his son's private studies were leading. He writes to Victor with family news and worry when letters from home stop coming."}, {"id": "caroline-beaufort", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["Caroline Beaufort", "Caroline Frankenstein"], "subtitle": "Victor's mother", "body": "Daughter of a ruined friend of Alphonse's, taken in and later married by him after her father's death. Her own death, from scarlet fever caught nursing Elizabeth, falls on Victor just before he leaves for university."}, {"id": "elizabeth", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "major", "aliases": ["Elizabeth Lavenza", "Elizabeth"], "subtitle": "An orphan raised as Victor's cousin and intended bride", "body": "Brought into the Frankenstein household as a small child and raised alongside Victor, who from early on regards her as more than a sister. She stays close to the family through every absence and misfortune that follows."}, {"id": "henry-clerval", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "major", "aliases": ["Henry Clerval", "Clerval", "Henry"], "subtitle": "Victor's closest friend from childhood", "body": "A merchant's son with a taste for poetry and adventure rather than the family business, he follows Victor to Ingolstadt and nurses him through a long illness there. His warmth is a steady contrast to Victor's growing secrecy."}, {"id": "william", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["William Frankenstein", "little William"], "subtitle": "Victor's youngest brother", "body": "A cherished child in the Frankenstein family, still young enough to be spoiled by everyone around him. News concerning him is what finally calls Victor back home from his travels with Clerval."}, {"id": "justine", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "major", "aliases": ["Justine Moritz", "Justine"], "subtitle": "A servant raised almost as a member of the family", "body": "Taken in by Caroline Frankenstein as a girl after trouble at her own home, she becomes devoted to the family, especially to William. She returns to Geneva to visit her mother on the night William goes missing."}, {"id": "ernest", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "reference", "aliases": ["Ernest Frankenstein", "Ernest"], "subtitle": "Victor's middle brother", "body": "The second Frankenstein son, mentioned in family letters as being intended for a soldier's career against his father's preference for the law. He remains in Geneva through the family's troubles."}, {"id": "de-lacey", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["De Lacey", "the old man", "the old De Lacey"], "subtitle": "A blind old man living in exile in a cottage", "body": "Once a comfortable Parisian, now reduced to a simple rural cottage with his two children, supported by a son's labor and a daughter's care. His blindness makes him the one person the creature can approach without being fled from on sight."}, {"id": "felix", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["Felix"], "subtitle": "De Lacey's son", "body": "A young man carrying his family's poverty and a private grief the creature slowly pieces together by watching the cottage. He works the land and teaches his father's household through hardships that once knew better days."}, {"id": "agatha", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["Agatha"], "subtitle": "De Lacey's daughter", "body": "Felix's sister, who shares the daily work and quiet sorrow of the cottage. Her gentleness toward her blind father is part of what teaches the creature what a family can be."}, {"id": "safie", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "supporting", "aliases": ["Safie"], "subtitle": "An Arabian woman who joins the De Lacey household", "body": "The daughter of a Turkish merchant, she arrives at the cottage seeking Felix, to whom she is devoted, and stays to learn the family's language. Her lessons, overheard by the creature in hiding, become his own education in speech and history."}, {"id": "krempe", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "reference", "aliases": ["M. Krempe", "Krempe"], "subtitle": "A professor of natural philosophy at Ingolstadt", "body": "A blunt, unimpressive-looking professor who dismisses Victor's early reading in old alchemical writers as useless. His scorn stings Victor without turning him from science itself."}, {"id": "waldman", "kind": "person", "storyRole": "reference", "aliases": ["M. Waldman", "Waldman"], "subtitle": "A professor of chemistry at Ingolstadt", "body": "Kind where Krempe was dismissive, he reframes the old alchemists as flawed pioneers of real modern science and encourages Victor's fascination rather than mocking it. His lectures are what finally set Victor's private research in motion."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Letter 1", "paragraphs": ["_To Mrs. Saville, England._", "St. Petersburgh, Dec. 11th, 17—.", "You will rejoice to hear that no disaster has accompanied the commencement of an enterprise which you have regarded with such evil forebodings. I arrived here yesterday, and my first task is to assure my dear sister of my welfare and increasing confidence in the success of my undertaking."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Letter 1", "paragraphs": ["_To Mrs. Saville, England._", "St. Petersburg, December 11th, 17—.", "You'll be glad to hear that nothing has gone wrong at the start of this expedition you were so worried about. I arrived here yesterday. My first task is to reassure my dear sister that I'm well and more confident than ever in the success of my venture."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Brev 1", "paragraphs": ["_Til Mrs. Saville, England._", "Sankt Petersborg, 11. december, 17—.", "Det vil glæde dig at høre, at intet er gået galt i starten af denne ekspedition, som du var så bekymret for. Jeg ankom hertil i går. Min første opgave er at berolige min kære søster med, at jeg har det godt og er mere overbevist end nogensinde om, at mit forehavende vil lykkes."]}}, "cover": "assets/frankenstein.jpg", "coverTone": "#20352b"}, {"id": "jekyll-and-hyde", "preface": ["The world of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde is full of men who know when not to ask a question. Friendship, professional standing, and ordinary discretion depend on allowing certain doors to remain closed. Stevenson makes this respectable restraint the setting for a mystery in which silence becomes increasingly difficult to distinguish from protection of something dangerous.", "The story is approached through observers trying to make sense of incomplete evidence. A document, an unexplained association, or a disturbing encounter offers a fragment whose significance is not yet clear. The familiar names in the title can obscure how carefully the book arranges the experience of uncertainty.", "Its London has an extraordinary compactness. Streets and interiors carry different moral atmospheres, while neighbouring spaces can seem to belong to separate lives. The city's arrangements give secrecy a physical form: a person may be well known from one entrance and scarcely recognised from another.", "Beneath the suspense is an interest in the wish to separate conduct from consequence. Reputation encourages people to identify with the part of themselves that receives approval, while other desires are treated as though they belong to someone else. Stevenson takes that habit seriously enough to explore its imaginative possibilities.", "The novella's brevity strengthens its effect. There is little room for the unease to disperse, and every attempt at explanation seems to expose another difficulty. Its enduring subject is the work required to preserve an agreeable account of oneself, especially when the private life has begun making demands that the public identity cannot acknowledge."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Gabriel Utterson"], "subtitle": "The Investigating Lawyer", "body": "A respectable London lawyer, old friend of Jekyll, and the story's primary viewpoint character. He is quiet, methodical, loyal, and deeply uneasy. The story follows his investigation into Jekyll's connection with Hyde, and his growing dread is the emotional register of the book. He represents the Victorian professional class at its most decent — and its most limited in the face of what it cannot explain."}, {"aliases": ["Dr. Henry Jekyll"], "subtitle": "The Divided Man", "body": "A successful physician and scientist, well-liked, generous, sociable — and concealing an inner life he considers incompatible with his public reputation. His experiment is not an accident but a deliberate attempt to separate the respectable and disreputable halves of his nature. He does not want to destroy Hyde; he wants to indulge Hyde without consequences. The discovery that this is impossible is the book's central horror."}, {"aliases": ["Edward Hyde"], "subtitle": "The Other Self", "body": "The figure Jekyll's compound releases: small, young, physically repulsive in a way no one can quite articulate. He is not a separate person but a distillation of everything Jekyll has suppressed. He tramples a child, murders a member of Parliament, and moves through London with a freedom that is itself a form of violence. Everyone who meets him feels an instinctive revulsion — as if the body recognizes what the mind has not yet named."}, {"aliases": ["Dr. Hastie Lanyon"], "subtitle": "The Rational Witness", "body": "A physician and former close friend of Jekyll who broke with him over what he considered Jekyll's unscientific speculations. When he finally witnesses the truth — a transformation he cannot deny and cannot explain — the shock kills him. His death is the book's most pointed argument: that there are things the rational, professional Victorian mind is not equipped to survive."}, {"aliases": ["Mr. Enfield"], "subtitle": "The First Witness", "body": "Utterson's distant kinsman and walking companion, who starts the entire investigation by telling the story of a door — a door through which he once saw a small man trample a child. His account is the book's opening mystery, and his instinct to suppress the details rather than pursue them is the first sign of the culture of discretion the book will dissect."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Story of the Door", "paragraphs": ["Mr. Utterson the lawyer was a man of a rugged countenance that was never lighted by a smile; cold, scanty and embarrassed in discourse; backward in sentiment; lean, long, dusty, dreary and yet somehow lovable. At friendly meetings, and when the wine was to his taste, something eminently human beaconed from his eye; something indeed which never found its way into his talk, but which spoke not only in these silent symbols of the after-dinner face, but more often and loudly in the acts of his life. He was austere with himself; drank gin when he was alone, to mortify a taste for vintages; and though he enjoyed the theatre, had not crossed the doors of one for twenty years. But he had an approved tolerance for others; sometimes wondering, almost with envy, at the high pressure of spirits involved in their misdeeds; and in any extremity inclined to help rather than to reprove. “I incline to Cain’s heresy,” he used to say quaintly: “I let my brother go to the devil in his own way.” In this character, it was frequently his fortune to be the last reputable acquaintance and the last good influence in the lives of downgoing men. And to such as these, so long as they came about his chambers, he never marked a shade of change in his demeanour.", "No doubt the feat was easy to Mr. Utterson; for he was undemonstrative at the best, and even his friendship seemed to be founded in a similar catholicity of good-nature. It is the mark of a modest man to accept his friendly circle ready-made from the hands of opportunity; and that was the lawyer’s way. His friends were those of his own blood or those whom he had known the longest; his affections, like ivy, were the growth of time, they implied no aptness in the object. Hence, no doubt the bond that united him to Mr. Richard Enfield, his distant kinsman, the well-known man about town. It was a nut to crack for many, what these two could see in each other, or what subject they could find in common. It was reported by those who encountered them in their Sunday walks, that they said nothing, looked singularly dull and would hail with obvious relief the appearance of a friend. For all that, the two men put the greatest store by these excursions, counted them the chief jewel of each week, and not only set aside occasions of pleasure, but even resisted the calls of business, that they might enjoy them uninterrupted.", "It chanced on one of these rambles that their way led them down a by-street in a busy quarter of London. The street was small and what is called quiet, but it drove a thriving trade on the weekdays. The inhabitants were all doing well, it seemed, and all emulously hoping to do better still, and laying out the surplus of their gains in coquetry; so that the shop fronts stood along that thoroughfare with an air of invitation, like rows of smiling saleswomen. Even on Sunday, when it veiled its more florid charms and lay comparatively empty of passage, the street shone out in contrast to its dingy neighbourhood, like a fire in a forest; and with its freshly painted shutters, well-polished brasses, and general cleanliness and gaiety of note, instantly caught and pleased the eye of the passenger."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Story of the Door", "paragraphs": ["Mr. Utterson the lawyer was a man of rough features that never lit up with a smile—cold, sparing, and awkward in conversation; reserved in feeling; lean, long, dusty, dreary, and yet somehow lovable. At friendly gatherings, when the wine suited his taste, something deeply human shone from his eye—something that never made it into his speech, but spoke not only in the silent signs on his post-dinner face, but louder and more often in the actions of his life. He was strict with himself: he drank gin alone, to discipline his taste for fine wines; and though he enjoyed the theatre, he had not crossed the door of one in twenty years. But he extended a settled tolerance to others—sometimes wondering, almost with envy, at the wild energy that drove their misdeeds; and in any extremity he was inclined to help rather than condemn. “I lean toward Cain’s heresy,” he used to say in his quaint way: “I let my brother go to the devil in his own way.” In this role it was often his lot to be the last respectable acquaintance and the last good influence in the lives of men who were going down. And toward such men, as long as they came to his rooms, he never showed the slightest change in his manner.", "This was no doubt easy for Mr. Utterson, since he was undemonstrative at the best of times, and even his friendships seemed founded on a similar broad good-nature. It is the mark of a modest man to accept his circle of friends ready-made from the hands of chance; and that was the lawyer’s way. His friends were either blood relations or men he had known the longest; his affections, like ivy, were the growth of time, and implied nothing special about their object. Hence, no doubt, the bond between him and Mr. Richard Enfield, his distant kinsman, the well-known man about town. What these two could see in each other, or what subject they could find in common, was a puzzle to many. People who met them on their Sunday walks reported that they said nothing, looked unusually dull, and would greet the appearance of a friend with obvious relief. Still, the two men placed the highest value on these outings, counted them the chief jewel of each week, and not only put aside chances for pleasure but even resisted business calls so they could enjoy them uninterrupted.", "On one of these rambles, their walk happened to lead them down a side street in a busy quarter of London. The street was small and what people called quiet, but on weekdays it did a thriving trade. The residents all seemed to be doing well, and all were eager to do better still, spending the surplus of their earnings on display; so the shop fronts along that thoroughfare stood with an inviting air, like rows of smiling saleswomen. Even on Sunday, when the street veiled its more showy charms and lay comparatively empty of traffic, it shone out against its grimy neighbourhood like a fire in a forest; and with its freshly painted shutters, well-polished brasswork, and overall cleanliness and cheerfulness, it instantly caught and pleased the eye of any passerby."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Historien om døren", "paragraphs": ["Mr. Utterson, sagføreren, var en mand med barske træk, som aldrig lyste op i et smil — kølig, sparsom og kejtet i samtale, tilbageholdende i følelse, mager, lang, støvet, kedsommelig og dog på en eller anden måde elskværdig. Ved venlige sammenkomster, når vinen var efter hans smag, lyste der noget dybt menneskeligt ud af hans øje — noget, der aldrig kom til orde i hans tale, men talte ikke kun i de tavse tegn i hans ansigt efter middagen, men højere og oftere i hans livs handlinger. Han var streng med sig selv: han drak gin, når han var alene, for at tugte sin smag for fine vine; og selv om han nød teatret, havde han ikke sat sin fod på et i tyve år. Men over for andre udviste han en grundfæstet tolerance — undertiden undrede han sig, næsten med misundelse, over den vilde energi, der drev deres misgerninger; og i enhver yderlighed var han mere tilbøjelig til at hjælpe end at fordømme. »Jeg hælder mod Kains kætteri,« plejede han at sige på sin kuriøse vis: »jeg lader min broder gå til djævelen på sin egen måde.« I denne rolle blev det ofte hans lod at være den sidste agtværdige bekendtskab og den sidste gode indflydelse i livet for mænd, der var på vej ned. Og over for sådanne mænd, så længe de kom i hans gemakker, viste han aldrig den mindste ændring i sin manér.", "Dette var uden tvivl let for Mr. Utterson, for han var udemonstrativ i sit bedste, og selv hans venskaber syntes at hvile på en lignende bred godmodighed. Det er en beskeden mands kendetegn, at han accepterer sin vennekreds, som tilfældet har bragt ham den; og sådan var sagførerens måde. Hans venner var enten blodsbeslægtede eller dem, han havde kendt længst; hans følelser var som vedbend — tidens vækst, og indebar ikke noget særligt ved deres genstand. Heraf vel også båndet mellem ham og Mr. Richard Enfield, hans fjerne slægtning, den velkendte mand om bord. Hvad disse to kunne se hos hinanden, eller hvilket emne de kunne finde fælles, var en gåde for mange. Folk, der mødte dem på deres søndagsspadsereture, fortalte, at de ikke sagde noget, så usædvanlig kedelige ud og hilste mødet med en bekendt med tydelig lettelse. Alligevel satte de to mænd den højeste pris på disse udflugter, regnede dem for hver uges fineste juvel, og tilsidesatte ikke blot lejligheder til fornøjelse, men modstod endog forretningens kalden, for at de kunne nyde dem uforstyrret.", "Det skete på en af disse vandringer, at deres vej førte dem ned ad en sidegade i et travlt kvarter i London. Gaden var lille og hvad man kalder stille, men i hverdagene gik der en blomstrende handel. Beboerne lod alle til at have det godt, og alle håbede ivrigt at få det endnu bedre, idet de brugte overskuddet af deres fortjeneste på pynt; så butiksfacaderne langs den hovedfærdsel stod med en indbydende mine, som rækker af smilende ekspeditricer. Selv om søndagen, når gaden tilslørede sine mere prangende charmer og lå forholdsvis tom for trafik, skinnede den ud mod sit snavsede nabolag som en ild i en skov; og med sine nymalede skodder, velpolerede messingbeslag og en almindelig renlighed og munterhed fangede og glædede den straks enhver forbipasserendes blik."]}}, "title": "Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde", "author": "Robert Louis Stevenson", "crop": [779, 1010, 170, 243], "cover": "assets/jekyll-and-hyde.jpg", "coverTone": "#492a29"}, {"id": "candide", "preface": ["Candide has been taught that the world is arranged for the best. Experience proves a remarkably energetic opponent of this education. Misfortune follows misfortune, while explanations remain available in almost unlimited supply. Voltaire finds a savage comedy in the distance between being able to account for suffering and having any intention of relieving it.", "The story moves with extraordinary speed. A catastrophe that another novelist might spend a volume exploring can arrive and be left behind within a page. The lightness of the telling makes the brutality more conspicuous. Institutions continue their business, learned people continue explaining, and those with something to gain discover that events confirm their principles.", "Voltaire's targets include religious cruelty, military glory, colonial exploitation, and the comfortable language in which these can be defended. His impatience is directed especially at a habit of thought: beginning with a conclusion about the goodness of the arrangement and fitting other people's pain into it afterwards.", "Candide himself gives this attack its peculiar innocence. His readiness to hope is often absurd, yet a world entirely rid of hope would offer little reason to care what happens in it. The book can mock his education without making every form of trust contemptible. Its travelling company keeps the argument in contact with hunger, work, attachment, and the desire for somewhere to live.", "The humour is dark enough to require no pretence that everything is harmless. Voltaire makes laughter an instrument for refusing explanations that have become too pleased with themselves. Beneath the extravagant adventures is a practical concern about how to live once an elegant theory has stopped being an adequate response to another person's misery."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Candide"], "subtitle": "The Innocent", "body": "The illegitimate nephew of a Westphalian baron, raised on Pangloss's optimism and in love with the baron's daughter. He is gentle, credulous, and durable in a way that becomes almost comic — he survives flogging, shipwreck, the Inquisition, and the loss of nearly everyone he loves while trying to keep believing his tutor was right. His gradual disenchantment is the novel's spine. By the last chapter he has stopped quoting Pangloss and started planting onions."}, {"aliases": ["Pangloss"], "subtitle": "The Philosopher", "body": "Voltaire's parody of Leibniz, a tutor who teaches \"metaphysico-theologo-cosmolo-nigology\" and the doctrine that everything has a sufficient reason for being precisely as it is. He survives syphilis from a chambermaid, hanging at the Lisbon auto-da-fé, dissection by an anatomist, and slavery in the Ottoman galleys, and concludes from each disaster that this is still the best of all possible worlds. His optimism is not stupidity — it is the specific intellectual failure of a system that cannot be falsified by any evidence."}, {"aliases": ["Cunégonde"], "subtitle": "The Beloved", "body": "The baron's daughter, fresh and rosy and the first thing Candide loses. The novel reports what happens to her with brutal economy: her parents murdered, her body raped by soldiers, her person sold from a merchant to a Grand Inquisitor and finally to a Turkish prince. Candide spends the novel trying to find her. When he does, she is ugly and ill-tempered, and he marries her anyway. Voltaire's joke is that the object of romantic idealism is also a person, and people do not stay what the lover imagined."}, {"aliases": ["Cacambo"], "subtitle": "The Pragmatist", "body": "Candide's valet, picked up in Cadiz: a former Jesuit chorister of mixed Spanish and Inca descent. He speaks several languages, knows how to bribe and bargain, and consistently solves the problem in front of him while Candide and Pangloss philosophize. He is the one who navigates them through Paraguay and into El Dorado. He is loyal, inventive, and ironic, and the closest thing in the novel to someone Voltaire wholly admires."}, {"aliases": ["Martin"], "subtitle": "The Pessimist", "body": "A Manichaean scholar Candide selects on the voyage from Suriname specifically because his bleak view of the world will balance Pangloss's. Martin believes the earth has been abandoned to a malevolent power and that suffering is not a glitch in providence but its operating principle. Almost every prediction he makes turns out correct, which is its own kind of problem. He is right and useless, while Cacambo is partly wrong and effective. The novel takes that contrast seriously."}, {"aliases": ["The Old Woman"], "subtitle": "The Survivor", "body": "Cunégonde's servant on the run from Lisbon. Her chapters of autobiography are the bleakest in the book: born the daughter of a pope and a princess, she has been raped by pirates, sold into slavery in Morocco, used as currency on a starving ship where her left buttock was cut off and eaten. She tells all of this without self-pity. She asks every passenger she meets whether they have not at some point wanted to kill themselves. Most have. She finds the question oddly clarifying, and she keeps living."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Chapter 1 — How Candide Was Brought Up in a Magnificent Castle, and How He Was Expelled Thence", "paragraphs": ["In a castle of Westphalia, belonging to the Baron of Thunder-ten-Tronckh, lived a youth, whom nature had endowed with the most gentle manners. His countenance was a true picture of his soul. He combined a true judgment with simplicity of spirit, which was the reason, I apprehend, of his being called Candide. The old servants of the family suspected him to have been the son of the Baron's sister, by a good, honest gentleman of the neighborhood, whom that young lady would never marry because he had been able to prove only seventy-one quarterings, the rest of his genealogical tree having been lost through the injuries of time.", "The Baron was one of the most powerful lords in Westphalia, for his castle had not only a gate, but windows. His great hall, even, was hung with tapestry. All the dogs of his farm-yards formed a pack of hounds at need; his grooms were his huntsmen; and the curate of the village was his grand almoner. They called him \"My Lord,\" and laughed at all his stories.", "The Baron's lady weighed about three hundred and fifty pounds, and was therefore a person of great consideration, and she did the honours of the house with a dignity that commanded still greater respect. Her daughter Cunegonde was seventeen years of age, fresh-coloured, comely, plump, and desirable. The Baron's son seemed to be in every respect worthy of his father. The Preceptor Pangloss[1] was the oracle of the family, and little Candide heard his lessons with all the good faith of his age and character."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Chapter 1 — How Candide Was Brought Up in a Magnificent Castle, and How He Was Expelled Thence", "paragraphs": ["In a castle in Westphalia belonging to the Baron of Thunder-ten-Tronckh, there lived a young man whom nature had endowed with the gentlest manners. His face was a true picture of his soul. He combined sound judgment with simplicity of spirit, which is the reason, I believe, that he was called Candide. The old servants of the household suspected he was the son of the Baron's sister by a kind and honest gentleman of the neighborhood, a man the young lady had refused to marry because he could prove only seventy-one quarterings of nobility, the rest of his family tree having been lost to the ravages of time.", "The Baron was one of the most powerful lords in Westphalia, for his castle had not only a gate but windows. His great hall was even hung with tapestry. All the dogs in his farmyards could be mustered into a pack of hounds when needed; his grooms served as his huntsmen; and the village curate was his grand almoner. They called him \"My Lord\" and laughed at all his jokes.", "The Baron's wife weighed about three hundred and fifty pounds and was therefore a woman of great consequence, and she did the honors of the house with a dignity that commanded even greater respect. Her daughter Cunegonde was seventeen, rosy, pretty, plump, and desirable. The Baron's son appeared in every way worthy of his father. The tutor Pangloss was the oracle of the household, and little Candide listened to his lessons with all the innocent trust of his age and character."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Kapitel 1 — Hvordan Candide blev opdraget i et prægtigt slot og fordrevet derfra", "paragraphs": ["På et slot i Westphalia, som tilhørte Baronen af Thunder-ten-Tronckh, levede der en ung mand, som naturen havde udstyret med de mildeste manerer. Hans ansigt var et tro billede af hans sjæl. Han forenede sund dømmekraft med enfoldighed i sindet, og det er grunden, tror jeg, til at han blev kaldt Candide. De gamle tjenestefolk på godset havde mistanke om, at han var søn af Baronens søster ved en venlig og agtværdig herre fra egnen — en mand, den unge dame havde nægtet at ægte, fordi han kun kunne bevise enoghalvfjerds adelige ahneled, idet resten af hans stamtræ var gået tabt i tidens tand.", "Baronen var en af de mægtigste herrer i Westphalia, for hans slot havde ikke blot en port, men også vinduer. Hans store hal var endda behængt med gobeliner. Samtlige hunde i hans ladegårde kunne ved behov samles til en hel hundemeute; hans staldkarle gjorde tjeneste som jægere, og landsbypræsten var hans storalmisseuddeler. De kaldte ham »Herre« og lo ad alle hans vittigheder.", "Baronens hustru vejede omkring trehundrede og halvtreds pund og var derfor en kvinde af betydelig vægt, og hun gjorde husets honnører med en værdighed, der krævede endnu større respekt. Hendes datter Cunegonde var sytten år, rødmosset, køn, buttet og attråværdig. Baronens søn syntes i alle henseender faderen værdig. Huslæreren Pangloss var husstandens orakel, og lille Candide lyttede til hans lektioner med hele sin alders og sit naturels uskyldige tillid."]}}, "title": "Candide", "author": "Voltaire", "crop": [599, 1010, 164, 243], "cover": "assets/candide.jpg", "coverTone": "#7b724c"}, {"id": "julius-caesar", "title": "Julius Caesar", "author": "William Shakespeare", "preface": ["Written around 1599, probably just before Hamlet, Julius Caesar was one of the first plays performed at the newly built Globe Theatre. It draws on Plutarch's Lives and is Shakespeare's most direct engagement with republican politics — the question of when, if ever, it is justified to overthrow a ruler. The play refuses to settle the question. Caesar is arrogant but not a tyrant; Brutus is honorable but wrong about the consequences; Antony is opportunistic but effective. Shakespeare gives every position its strongest voice and lets the audience decide. The result is a play that has been claimed by revolutionaries and conservatives alike for four centuries — and that speaks to any moment when political violence is debated."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Brutus"], "subtitle": "The Honorable Conspirator", "body": "A Roman senator, respected by all, who joins the conspiracy against Caesar out of genuine concern for the republic rather than personal ambition. He is the play's moral center and its most complete failure — a man whose every principled decision produces a worse outcome than the unprincipled alternative would have. Shakespeare makes him sympathetic and wrong at the same time."}, {"aliases": ["Julius Caesar"], "subtitle": "The Dictator", "body": "The most powerful man in Rome, recently returned from military triumph, offered the crown three times by Antony and three times refusing it. He is proud, deaf in one ear, superstitious, and aware that men around him might wish him dead. He appears in fewer scenes than the title suggests; his assassination in Act 3 is the fulcrum of the play, and his ghost haunts the rest of it."}, {"aliases": ["Mark Antony"], "subtitle": "Caesar's Avenger", "body": "Caesar's loyal friend, initially dismissed by the conspirators as a mere follower. His funeral oration — 'Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears' — is the most famous speech in Shakespeare and the play's decisive turning point. He is politically shrewd, emotionally volatile, and ruthless once he has the crowd behind him."}, {"aliases": ["Cassius"], "subtitle": "The Instigator", "body": "The driving force behind the conspiracy, motivated by personal envy as much as political principle. He is a keen reader of other men — it is Cassius who recruits Brutus, knowing that the conspiracy needs Brutus's reputation to succeed. He is the more practical of the two leaders and is overruled by Brutus at every critical decision, to their mutual destruction."}, {"aliases": ["Portia"], "subtitle": "Brutus's Wife", "body": "Brutus's wife, who senses his inner turmoil and demands to know what conspiracy he is hiding. She wounds herself in the thigh to prove she can bear pain and keep secrets. Her death — reported later in the play — is one of the costs of the assassination that Brutus bears in private."}, {"aliases": ["Calpurnia"], "subtitle": "Caesar's Wife", "body": "Caesar's wife, who dreams of his murder the night before the Ides of March and begs him not to go to the Senate. Caesar almost listens — then is persuaded otherwise by the conspirators. Her warning is the play's clearest signal that the assassination could have been avoided."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Act 1, Scene 1 — Rome. A street", "paragraphs": ["Enter Flavius, Marullus and a throng of Citizens.", "FLAVIUS. Hence! home, you idle creatures, get you home. Is this a holiday? What, know you not, Being mechanical, you ought not walk Upon a labouring day without the sign Of your profession? Speak, what trade art thou?", "CARPENTER. Why, sir, a carpenter.", "MARULLUS. Where is thy leather apron and thy rule? What dost thou with thy best apparel on? You, sir, what trade are you?", "COBBLER. Truly, sir, in respect of a fine workman, I am but, as you would say, a cobbler.", "MARULLUS. But what trade art thou? Answer me directly."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Act 1, Scene 1 — Rome. A street", "paragraphs": ["[Enter Flavius, Marullus, and a throng of Citizens.]", "FLAVIUS. Get off the streets! Go home, you lazy creatures, go home. Is this a holiday? Don't you know that as working men you shouldn't walk around on a workday without the marks of your trade? Speak up — what's your trade?", "CARPENTER. Why, sir, I'm a carpenter.", "MARULLUS. Where's your leather apron and your ruler? What are you doing in your best clothes? And you, sir — what's your trade?", "COBBLER. Honestly, sir, compared to a fine workman, I'm only what you'd call a cobbler.", "MARULLUS. But what trade are you? Answer me straight."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Akt 1, Scene 1 — Rom. En gade", "paragraphs": ["[Flavius, Marullus og en flok borgere træder ind.]", "FLAVIUS. Væk fra gaderne! Gå hjem, I dovne væsener, gå hjem. Er det helligdag? Ved I ikke, at I som arbejdsmænd ikke bør gå rundt på en hverdag uden tegnene på jeres håndværk? Sig frem — hvad er dit håndværk?", "TØMRER. Jo, herre, jeg er tømrer.", "MARULLUS. Hvor er dit læderforklæde og din tommestok? Hvad laver du i dit fineste tøj? Og du, herre — hvad er dit håndværk?", "SKOMAGER. Ærligt talt, herre, sammenlignet med en dygtig håndværker er jeg kun det, man kalder en lappeskomager.", "MARULLUS. Men hvilket håndværk har du? Svar mig lige ud."]}}, "cover": "assets/julius-caesar.jpg", "coverTone": "#49302e", "typeset": true}, {"id": "the-prince", "title": "The Prince", "author": "Niccolò Machiavelli", "preface": ["Machiavelli wrote the book at his small farm at Sant'Andrea in Percussina, seven miles outside Florence. The Medici had returned to power in 1512 and dismissed him from his post as Second Chancellor of the Republic. Early in 1513 he was tortured on suspicion of conspiracy, released, and exiled. The Prince was an attempt to demonstrate his usefulness to the new regime and possibly get a job. He dedicated it to Lorenzo de' Medici and never received a position. The book circulated in manuscript for two decades and was not printed until 1532, five years after his death.", "It was placed on the Index of Forbidden Books in 1559 and remained there until 1890. The adjective \"Machiavellian\" entered English in the 1560s and immediately became an insult. Frederick the Great wrote a refutation of it as crown prince, then ruled like its most attentive student. Napoleon kept a copy with marginal notes. The book has been read as a cynical handbook, as an honest description of politics, and as a secret defense of republican liberty. It is short, under 30,000 words, and reads like a man who has watched a great deal of power up close and refuses to flatter what he saw."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Niccolò Machiavelli"], "subtitle": "The Author in Exile", "body": "Forty-four when he wrote the book, dismissed after fourteen years as Second Chancellor of the Florentine Republic, briefly tortured on suspicion of conspiracy, and living on a small farm seven miles from Florence. He composed The Prince in the evenings, after spending the days at the local inn. A famous letter to Francesco Vettori describes him changing into formal clothes at sundown to enter \"the courts of the ancients\" and write. The voice of the book is the voice of a man who has lost his career and refuses to lose his observations."}, {"aliases": ["Cesare Borgia"], "subtitle": "The Working Model", "body": "Son of Pope Alexander VI. Used his father's patronage to carve a personal state out of the Romagna between 1499 and 1503 through a combination of diplomacy, betrayal, calculated cruelty, and one famous massacre at Senigallia. Machiavelli met him three times as a Florentine envoy and watched him at work. Cesare's state collapsed within a year of his father's death, but Machiavelli treats him in Chapter VII as the closest contemporary model of how a new prince actually consolidates power, naming his single error: not having ensured a friendly successor in the papacy."}, {"aliases": ["The New Prince"], "subtitle": "The Central Subject", "body": "Not a hereditary ruler with centuries of legitimacy behind him but someone who has just seized power and now has to keep it. He is Machiavelli's real subject, the hardest case, the figure who must build everything without inherited authority to lean on. The book is structured around his problems: how to handle the conquered population, when to act decisively, when to delay, what to do with mercenaries, how to manage advisors, how to be feared without being hated. Hereditary princes get one short chapter. The new prince gets the rest."}, {"aliases": ["The Armed Prophet"], "subtitle": "The Founder Type", "body": "Machiavelli's category for the rulers who do not just take power but found new orders: Moses, Romulus, Theseus, Cyrus. He notes coldly in Chapter VI that all of them succeeded because they were armed, while Savonarola, the Florentine friar who tried to refound the republic on prophetic authority alone, was hanged and burned in the Piazza della Signoria in 1498. The point is not that prophecy is empty. The point is that ideas without force do not survive contact with the people who are losing under them. Unarmed prophets come to ruin."}, {"aliases": ["Hannibal"], "subtitle": "The Discipline Of Cruelty", "body": "Machiavelli's example in Chapter XVII of how cruelty well used keeps an army together. Hannibal led a polyglot force of Africans, Spaniards, and Gauls through Italy for sixteen years without a single mutiny — Machiavelli attributes this to the \"inhuman cruelty\" that ran alongside his other virtues, because it produced the respect on which a multi-ethnic army depended. Set against Scipio, whose excessive mercy provoked a rebellion of his own troops in Spain. The pairing argues that in a leader of armies, discipline applied with severity is itself a kind of order."}, {"aliases": ["Fortuna"], "subtitle": "The Tide Of Events", "body": "The force in events no ruler controls — the death of a pope, the arrival of a foreign army, the timing of plague. Machiavelli devotes Chapter XXV to her and arrives at a famous image: she is like a flooding river. You cannot stop her, but you can build embankments in the years she is calm so that when she rises she does less damage. She rules half of what happens to a prince. The remaining half is virtù, and preparing for the flood is the closest the book comes to a philosophy of action."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Dedication: To Lorenzo de' Medici", "paragraphs": ["To the Magnificent Lorenzo Di Piero De’ Medici", "Those who strive to obtain the good graces of a prince are accustomed to come before him with such things as they hold most precious, or in which they see him take most delight; whence one often sees horses, arms, cloth of gold, precious stones, and similar ornaments presented to princes, worthy of their greatness.", "Desiring therefore to present myself to your Magnificence with some testimony of my devotion towards you, I have not found among my possessions anything which I hold more dear than, or value so much as, the knowledge of the actions of great men, acquired by long experience in contemporary affairs, and a continual study of antiquity; which, having reflected upon it with great and prolonged diligence, I now send, digested into a little volume, to your Magnificence.", "And although I may consider this work unworthy of your countenance, nevertheless I trust much to your benignity that it may be acceptable, seeing that it is not possible for me to make a better gift than to offer you the opportunity of understanding in the shortest time all that I have learnt in so many years, and with so many troubles and dangers; which work I have not embellished with swelling or magnificent words, nor stuffed with rounded periods, nor with any extrinsic allurements or adornments whatever, with which so many are accustomed to embellish their works; for I have wished either that no honour should be given it, or else that the truth of the matter and the weightiness of the theme shall make it acceptable.", "Nor do I hold with those who regard it as a presumption if a man of low and humble condition dare to discuss and settle the concerns of princes; because, just as those who draw landscapes place themselves below in the plain to contemplate the nature of the mountains and of lofty places, and in order to contemplate the plains place themselves upon high mountains, even so to understand the nature of the people it needs to be a prince, and to understand that of princes it needs to be of the people.", "Take then, your Magnificence, this little gift in the spirit in which I send it; wherein, if it be diligently read and considered by you, you will learn my extreme desire that you should attain that greatness which fortune and your other attributes promise. And if your Magnificence from the summit of your greatness will sometimes turn your eyes to these lower regions, you will see how unmeritedly I suffer a great and continued malignity of fortune."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Dedication: To Lorenzo de' Medici", "paragraphs": ["To the Magnificent Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici", "Those who wish to win a prince's favor customarily come before him with whatever they hold most precious, or whatever they see him take most delight in. So one often sees princes presented with horses, arms, cloth of gold, precious stones, and similar ornaments worthy of their greatness.", "Wishing, then, to present myself to Your Magnificence with some testimony of my devotion, I have found nothing among my possessions that I hold dearer or value more than my knowledge of the actions of great men — acquired through long experience of contemporary affairs and continual study of antiquity. I have reflected on this knowledge with great and prolonged diligence, and now send it, condensed into a small volume, to Your Magnificence.", "Although I may consider this work unworthy of your notice, I trust in your kindness that it will nevertheless be acceptable, since I cannot make a better gift than to offer you the chance to understand in the shortest time all that I have learned in so many years and through so many troubles and dangers. I have not embellished this work with swelling or magnificent phrases, nor stuffed it with rounded periods or any other extrinsic allurements or adornments with which so many writers decorate their works. I wished either that it should receive no honor at all, or that the truth of the matter and the weight of the theme alone should make it acceptable.", "Nor do I hold with those who consider it presumption for a man of low and humble condition to dare to discuss and settle the concerns of princes. Just as those who draw landscapes place themselves below in the plain to contemplate the nature of mountains and high places, and climb the high mountains to contemplate the plains, so to understand the nature of the people it takes a prince, and to understand that of princes it takes one of the people.", "Take, then, Your Magnificence, this small gift in the spirit in which I send it. If you read and consider it with diligence, you will see my extreme desire that you attain the greatness that fortune and your other qualities promise. And if Your Magnificence, from the summit of your greatness, will sometimes turn your eyes to these lower regions, you will see how undeservedly I suffer a great and continual malignity of fortune."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Dedikation: Til Lorenzo de' Medici", "paragraphs": ["Til den Højvelbårne Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici", "De, der ønsker at vinde en fyrstes gunst, træder sædvanligvis frem for ham med det, de selv holder dyrebarest, eller med det, de ser ham finde størst glæde ved. Derfor ser man ofte fyrster blive forærede heste, våben, gyldne klædestoffer, ædle stene og lignende prydelser, som er deres storhed værdig.", "Idet jeg da ønsker at vise mig for Deres Højvelbårenhed med et vidnesbyrd om min hengivenhed, har jeg blandt mine ejendele intet fundet, jeg holder kærere eller vurderer højere end mit kendskab til store mænds handlinger — vundet gennem lang erfaring med samtidens forhold og vedvarende studier af oldtiden. Jeg har overvejet denne kundskab med stor og langvarig flid, og sender den nu, sammenfattet i et lille bind, til Deres Højvelbårenhed.", "Skønt jeg måske selv betragter dette værk som uværdigt til Deres opmærksomhed, stoler jeg på Deres velvilje for at det dog vil blive modtaget; thi jeg kan ikke give nogen bedre gave end at tilbyde Dem chancen for på kortest tid at forstå alt det, jeg har lært gennem så mange år og gennem så mange besværligheder og farer. Jeg har ikke prydet dette værk med svulstige eller pragtfulde vendinger, ej heller fyldt det med afrundede perioder eller andre ydre lokkemidler eller udsmykninger, hvormed så mange forfattere udsmykker deres skrifter. Jeg ønskede enten, at det slet ikke skulle modtage nogen ære, eller at sagens sandhed og emnets tyngde alene skulle gøre det værdigt til at modtages.", "Heller ikke deler jeg de menneskers mening, som finder det formasteligt, at en mand af lav og ringe stand vover at drøfte og afgøre fyrsters anliggender. Ligesom de, der tegner landskaber, stiller sig nede på sletten for at betragte bjergenes og højdernes natur, og bestiger de høje bjerge for at betragte sletterne, således kræves der en fyrste for at forstå folkets natur, og en mand af folket for at forstå fyrsternes.", "Modtag da, Deres Højvelbårenhed, denne lille gave i den ånd, hvori jeg sender den. Hvis De læser den og overvejer den med flid, vil De se mit yderste ønske om, at De når den storhed, som lykken og Deres øvrige egenskaber lover Dem. Og hvis Deres Højvelbårenhed fra Deres storheds tinde undertiden vil vende blikket mod disse lavere egne, vil De se, hvor ufortjent jeg lider en stor og vedvarende skæbnens ondskab."]}}, "cover": "assets/the-prince.jpg", "coverTone": "#484b30", "typeset": true}, {"id": "meditations", "preface": ["Marcus Aurelius repeatedly reminds himself to be patient with difficult people. The repetition is part of what makes Meditations affecting. An emperor could command armies, appoint officials, and punish an insult, yet still needed to work on getting out of bed, accepting criticism, and resisting the pleasure of feeling aggrieved. Authority had not relieved him of the ordinary labour of managing a mind.", "These notes were written for his own use, which gives them a different character from advice prepared for an audience. He returns to the same problems because knowing an argument is not the same as being able to live by it. A conviction that seems clear in a quiet moment has to survive fatigue, praise, disappointment, and the behaviour of colleagues.", "The Stoic discipline behind the writing asks him to distinguish his judgments and actions from the many things he cannot secure. Reputation, health, and other people's choices remain uncertain. His own conduct is where responsibility must begin. This can sound severe, particularly when he strips cherished things of their glamour, but it accompanies a persistent insistence on cooperation and service. Other people are the reason for the work, even when they make it unpleasant.", "His thoughts about death often serve the same practical purpose. A grievance looks different beside the brief lives of the people conducting it. So does the wish to be remembered by generations who will themselves vanish. The scale is meant to loosen an attachment to applause and return attention to what can be done now.", "There are flashes of unexpected tenderness in this austere exercise: affection for teachers, gratitude for example, pleasure in the irregular beauty of natural things. The book's companionship comes from meeting a person who keeps trying to bring his conduct closer to what he believes. He has considerable power and has not finished learning how to behave."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Marcus Aurelius"], "subtitle": "The Author", "body": "Roman emperor from 161 to 180 CE, philosopher-king in the literal Platonic sense. The Meditations is his private notebook from the wars on the Danube. He is reflective, exhausted, and self-critical — aware of his own anger, his vanity, his fatigue. The book is not the voice of a great man enthroned. It is the voice of a tired one alone in his tent, telling himself how to do tomorrow well. He died on campaign at fifty-eight, having written, almost certainly, with no idea anyone would ever read these notes."}, {"aliases": ["Antoninus Pius"], "subtitle": "The Model", "body": "Marcus's adoptive father and predecessor as emperor. The whole of Book I of the Meditations is a roll-call of people Marcus is grateful to, and the longest entry is for Antoninus. Marcus admired him for restraint, for stamina, for honesty in small things, for never hurrying. Antoninus is the human model behind the philosophy in the rest of the book — the proof that the discipline Marcus is drilling himself in is not theoretical, because Marcus had watched a man live it for twenty years."}, {"aliases": ["Epictetus"], "subtitle": "The Teacher Across Generations", "body": "A former slave who became one of the most influential Stoic teachers of the empire. Marcus never met him — Epictetus died about ten years before Marcus came to the throne — but his Discourses, transcribed by his student Arrian, were the working manual Marcus drew on. Epictetus's central distinction (what is up to us, what is not) runs through the Meditations like a refrain. He is, philosophically, Marcus's teacher across a generation."}, {"aliases": ["Junius Rusticus"], "subtitle": "The Mentor", "body": "The teacher Marcus thanks for, among other things, the gift of Epictetus's Discourses — the book that became the philosophical foundation of the Meditations. Rusticus also taught Marcus, by example, not to chase intellectual fashion, not to write showy treatises, not to perform virtue in public. Marcus credits him with showing what plain Stoic conduct actually looks like, in everyday speech and dress. He is the figure in Book I most directly responsible for the form the Meditations themselves take."}, {"aliases": ["Diognetus"], "subtitle": "The Early Teacher", "body": "One of Marcus's tutors in his youth, named in Book I. Marcus credits him with turning him toward philosophy, with teaching him not to be taken in by the impostures of magicians and miracle-workers, and with introducing him to the discipline of philosophical conversation. He stands in the book for the kind of teacher who, perhaps unwittingly, sets a young man on the road that ends decades later in the Meditations. Most of Marcus's gratitude is for influences this oblique and this lasting."}, {"aliases": ["The Logos"], "subtitle": "The Reason of the Universe", "body": "Not a person but the philosophical entity that runs through every page. The Stoics held that the cosmos was ordered by a rational principle — logos — and that the human mind, by being rational, participates in it. To live according to nature is to live according to logos. Marcus addresses logos directly in places, almost as a god, almost as a name for the structure of reality. The book's confidence that one's small efforts at sanity matter rests on this: that the cosmos is rationally arranged and one's reason can align with it."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Book 1", "paragraphs": ["1. From my grandfather Verus [I learned] good morals and the government of my temper.", "2. From the reputation and remembrance of my father, modesty and a manly character.", "3. From my mother, piety and beneficence, and abstinence, not only from evil deeds, but even from evil thoughts; and further, simplicity in my way of living, far removed from the habits of the rich."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Book 1", "paragraphs": ["1. From my grandfather Verus I learned good morals and the governing of my temper.", "2. From the reputation and memory of my father: modesty and a manly character.", "3. From my mother: piety and kindness, and to abstain not only from evil deeds but even from evil thoughts; and further, simplicity in my way of living, far removed from the habits of the rich."]}}, "title": "Meditations", "author": "Marcus Aurelius", "crop": [967, 1010, 169, 243], "cover": "assets/meditations.jpg", "coverTone": "#273744"}, {"id": "jane-eyre", "title": "Jane Eyre", "author": "Charlotte Brontë", "preface": ["Charlotte Brontë wrote it at home in Haworth, on the Yorkshire moors, while caring for her dying brother and watching her sisters write Wuthering Heights and Agnes Grey at the same parlor table. It was published in October 1847 under the pseudonym Currer Bell. It was an immediate critical and commercial success — three editions in six months — and the question of who Currer Bell actually was occupied literary London for two years. Thackeray, to whom Brontë later dedicated the second edition, sat up all night to finish it.", "The first-person voice — a poor, plain, intelligent woman insisting on her own moral worth — was new in 1847, and the novel's directness about a woman's interior life still gives it a shock English fiction has rarely equaled."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Jane Eyre"], "subtitle": "The Narrator", "body": "Plain, poor, and small, narrating her own life from ten to thirty. She tells the truth about herself in a way no Victorian heroine before her had been allowed to: about anger, about pride, about wanting to be loved on terms she will not compromise. She walks out into the rain rather than become Rochester's mistress, and walks back only when she has her own money and he has nothing left to bribe her with. She is one of the most distinctive narrators in English fiction."}, {"aliases": ["Edward Rochester"], "subtitle": "The Master of Thornfield", "body": "Dark, restless, ironic, twenty years older than Jane and heir to an estate he never wanted. He is hiding a wife in the third story of his own house. He falls in love with Jane partly because she refuses to flatter him and partly because she sees through him. He proposes, dresses her in jewels she does not want, and almost commits bigamy at the altar. By the end he has been blinded and maimed in the fire that burned Thornfield down. The novel is exact about his failures."}, {"aliases": ["Bertha Mason"], "subtitle": "The Wife in the Attic", "body": "Rochester's first wife: a Creole heiress from Spanish Town, Jamaica, whom he married at twenty-three and quickly came to fear. The novel describes her as mad and violent. She has been locked in the third story of Thornfield for ten years under a hired warden, escaping occasionally to set fires and bite. She is the source of the novel's gothic atmosphere and its deepest moral problem; she is never given her own voice. Jean Rhys's Wide Sargasso Sea would, a century later, attempt to give it back."}, {"aliases": ["St. John Rivers"], "subtitle": "Duty Without Warmth", "body": "The clergyman who finds Jane near death on the moors and turns out to be her unknown cousin. Beautiful, glacial, consumed by a missionary calling to India. He proposes to Jane on the explicit grounds that she would be useful to his work, not because he loves her — he frankly admits he does not. The novel's most frightening suitor, because his demands come wrapped in scripture. The chapter in which Jane comes within an inch of accepting him is the book's hardest test of her resolve."}, {"aliases": ["Helen Burns"], "subtitle": "The First Real Friend", "body": "An older girl at Lowood school who quietly endures floggings for blots on her copybook and unbrushed hair. Stoic, principled, devout, dying of consumption almost from the moment Jane meets her. She teaches Jane that some injustices have to be endured rather than answered, and that resentment is itself a kind of bondage. She dies in the same bed as Jane, holding her hand, in one of the most quoted scenes in Victorian fiction. Helen is based on Charlotte Brontë's older sister Maria, who died of tuberculosis at eleven."}, {"aliases": ["Mrs. Reed"], "subtitle": "The First Injustice", "body": "Jane's aunt by marriage, who took Jane in after her mother died and treated her as a parasite ever afterward. She locks ten-year-old Jane in the red-room — the room where her uncle died — until she has a fit. Years later, she lies to a potential benefactor about Jane's character. Jane confronts her twice: once at ten in a moment of explosive defiance, and once at her deathbed, where Jane chooses not to take revenge. The second confrontation is one of the great scenes in the novel."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Chapter 1", "paragraphs": ["There was no possibility of taking a walk that day. We had been wandering, indeed, in the leafless shrubbery an hour in the morning; but since dinner (Mrs. Reed, when there was no company, dined early) the cold winter wind had brought with it clouds so sombre, and a rain so penetrating, that further outdoor exercise was now out of the question.", "I was glad of it: I never liked long walks, especially on chilly afternoons: dreadful to me was the coming home in the raw twilight, with nipped fingers and toes, and a heart saddened by the chidings of Bessie, the nurse, and humbled by the consciousness of my physical inferiority to Eliza, John, and Georgiana Reed.", "The said Eliza, John, and Georgiana were now clustered round their mama in the drawing-room: she lay reclined on a sofa by the fireside, and with her darlings about her (for the time neither quarrelling nor crying) looked perfectly happy. Me, she had dispensed from joining the group; saying, “She regretted to be under the necessity of keeping me at a distance; but that until she heard from Bessie, and could discover by her own observation, that I was endeavouring in good earnest to acquire a more sociable and childlike disposition, a more attractive and sprightly manner—something lighter, franker, more natural, as it were—she really must exclude me from privileges intended only for contented, happy, little children.”", "“What does Bessie say I have done?” I asked.", "“Jane, I don’t like cavillers or questioners; besides, there is something truly forbidding in a child taking up her elders in that manner. Be seated somewhere; and until you can speak pleasantly, remain silent.”", "A breakfast-room adjoined the drawing-room, I slipped in there. It contained a bookcase: I soon possessed myself of a volume, taking care that it should be one stored with pictures. I mounted into the window-seat: gathering up my feet, I sat cross-legged, like a Turk; and, having drawn the red moreen curtain nearly close, I was shrined in double retirement."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Chapter 1", "paragraphs": ["There was no chance of going for a walk that day. We had wandered through the bare shrubbery for an hour that morning, but since lunch (Mrs. Reed dined early when there were no guests), the cold winter wind had brought such dark clouds and such relentless rain that any further time outdoors was out of the question.", "I was glad of it. I never liked long walks, especially on chilly afternoons. I dreaded coming home in the raw twilight with frozen fingers and toes, my heart heavy from Bessie the nurse's scoldings, and humbled by the awareness that I was physically inferior to Eliza, John, and Georgiana Reed.", "Eliza, John, and Georgiana were now gathered around their mama in the drawing room. She lay on a sofa by the fire, and with her darlings around her (for the moment neither fighting nor crying), she looked perfectly content. She had excused me from joining the group, saying that she regretted having to keep me at a distance, but that until she heard from Bessie, and could see for herself, that I was genuinely trying to develop a more sociable and childlike nature, a more appealing and lively manner—something lighter, more open, more natural—she really had to exclude me from privileges meant only for happy, well-behaved little children.", "\"What does Bessie say I have done?\" I asked.", "\"Jane, I don't like arguers or questioners. Besides, there is something truly unpleasant about a child challenging her elders like that. Sit down somewhere, and until you can speak pleasantly, stay quiet.\"", "A breakfast room adjoined the drawing room, and I slipped in there. It had a bookcase, and I quickly grabbed a book, making sure it was one full of pictures. I climbed into the window seat, tucked my feet up, and sat cross-legged. Having pulled the red curtain nearly shut, I was hidden away in double seclusion."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Kapitel 1", "paragraphs": ["Der var ingen mulighed for at gå en tur den dag. Vi havde vandret rundt i de nøgne buske en time den morgen, men siden frokost (Mrs. Reed spiste tidligt, når der ikke var gæster) havde den kolde vintervind bragt så mørke skyer og så ubarmhjertig regn, at mere tid udendørs var udelukket.", "Jeg var glad for det. Jeg brød mig aldrig om lange gåture, især ikke om kolde eftermiddage. Jeg frygtede at komme hjem i det rå tusmørke med forfrosne fingre og tæer, tynget af Bessies skæld ud, og ydmyget af bevidstheden om, at jeg fysisk var underlegen i forhold til Eliza, John og Georgiana Reed.", "Eliza, John og Georgiana var nu samlet om deres mama i dagligstuen. Hun lå på en sofa ved ilden, og med sine elskede børn omkring sig (som i øjeblikket hverken skændtes eller græd) så hun helt tilfreds ud. Hun havde fritaget mig fra at være med i gruppen og sagt, at hun beklagede at måtte holde mig på afstand, men at hun, indtil hun hørte fra Bessie og selv kunne se, at jeg oprigtigt forsøgte at udvikle et mere selskabeligt og barnligt væsen, en mere tiltalende og livlig optræden — noget lettere, mere åbent, mere naturligt — virkelig måtte udelukke mig fra privilegier, der kun var beregnet på glade, velopdragne børn.", "»Hvad siger Bessie, jeg har gjort?« spurgte jeg.", "»Jane, jeg kan ikke lide folk, der argumenterer eller stiller spørgsmål. Desuden er der noget virkelig ubehageligt ved et barn, der udfordrer sine ældre på den måde. Sæt dig et sted, og vær stille, indtil du kan tale pænt.«", "Et morgenmadsværelse lå ved siden af dagligstuen, og jeg smuttede derind. Der var en bogreol, og jeg greb hurtigt en bog og sørgede for, at den var fuld af billeder. Jeg kravlede op i vinduessædet, trak fødderne op og sad med korslagte ben. Efter at have trukket det røde gardin næsten helt for sad jeg gemt i dobbelt afsondrethed."]}}, "cover": "assets/jane-eyre.jpg", "coverTone": "#373644", "typeset": true}, {"id": "odyssey", "title": "The Odyssey", "author": "Homer", "preface": ["Composed orally in Greek around the 8th century BCE and attributed to Homer, the Odyssey is shorter than the Iliad and structured differently: episodic, told partly in flashback, and centered on a single man rather than a ten-year siege. The first four books follow Telemachus searching for news of his father. The next eight are Odysseus's wanderings, told around Calypso's hearth and at a feast in the court of the Phaeacians. The last twelve are the return to Ithaca and the bloody reclamation of his house.", "It is the founding adventure story in Western literature, and almost every adventure story since — Sinbad, the Aeneid, Dante's Inferno, Robinson Crusoe, Joyce's Ulysses, Star Wars — is in conversation with it. To read it only as adventure misses the harder argument underneath. The Odyssey is a serious treatment of cunning as a virtue, of what marriage means after twenty years apart, and of the duties a household owes to strangers who arrive unannounced. Odysseus does not come home as a hero who has earned his rest. He comes home disguised as a beggar; he is tested by his own wife; and the violence of his arrival is the price the poem charges for his absence."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Odysseus"], "subtitle": "The Cunning One", "body": "King of Ithaca, called polytropos — the man of many turns. Twenty years away from home: ten at Troy, ten trying to get back. He survives by lying, planning, disguising himself, and using words. The poem is named after him and follows him from Calypso's island all the way to a slaughter in his own dining hall."}, {"aliases": ["Penelope"], "subtitle": "His Wife", "body": "Twenty years of waiting. For the last three, she has held a hundred suitors at bay in her own house — telling them she will choose a husband when she finishes weaving a shroud for her father-in-law, and unweaving every night what she wove that day. She is the female equivalent of Odysseus: she survives by cunning. When he returns, she tests him too."}, {"aliases": ["Telemachus"], "subtitle": "The Son", "body": "Twenty years old when the poem begins; he has never known his father. He has grown up in a house full of armed men who eat his inheritance and threaten to kill him. The first four books are his — he leaves Ithaca to find news of Odysseus, and the journey is his coming-of-age. By the time his father returns, Telemachus is ready to fight at his side."}, {"aliases": ["Athena"], "subtitle": "Goddess of Wisdom", "body": "Odysseus's champion among the gods. She loves him because he thinks. She appears in disguise throughout the poem — as a young man, a shepherd, a beggar — testing those around her and steering events toward his return. She is the patron of cunning intelligence in the Greek world, and the Odyssey is in part her story too."}, {"aliases": ["Poseidon"], "subtitle": "God of the Sea", "body": "The antagonist. Odysseus blinded his son the Cyclops, and Poseidon spends the next ten years trying to drown him for it. The storms, the shipwrecks, the lost crew — most of what stands between Odysseus and home traces back to Poseidon's grudge. The poem opens with the gods debating whether he has paid enough."}, {"aliases": ["The Suitors"], "subtitle": "The Problem at Home", "body": "A hundred and eight young aristocrats from Ithaca and the neighboring islands, camped in Odysseus's palace for three years. They eat his food, drink his wine, sleep with his serving women, and compete to marry his wife. The poem makes you know them as individuals before Odysseus kills them, one by one — and that is part of its weight."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Book 1 — The gods in council—Minerva’s visit to Ithaca—the challenge from Telemachus to the suitors", "paragraphs": ["Tell me, O Muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide\nafter he had sacked the famous town of Troy. Many cities did he visit,\nand many were the nations with whose manners and customs he was\nacquainted; moreover he suffered much by sea while trying to save his\nown life and bring his men safely home; but do what he might he could\nnot save his men, for they perished through their own sheer folly in\neating the cattle of the Sun-god Hyperion; so the god prevented them\nfrom ever reaching home. Tell me, too, about all these things, oh\ndaughter of Jove, from whatsoever source you may know them.", "So now all who escaped death in battle or by shipwreck had got safely\nhome except Ulysses, and he, though he was longing to return to his\nwife and country, was detained by the goddess Calypso, who had got him\ninto a large cave and wanted to marry him. But as years went by, there\ncame a time when the gods settled that he should go back to Ithaca;\neven then, however, when he was among his own people, his troubles were\nnot yet over; nevertheless all the gods had now begun to pity him\nexcept Neptune, who still persecuted him without ceasing and would not\nlet him get home.", "Now Neptune had gone off to the Ethiopians, who are at the world’s end,\nand lie in two halves, the one looking West and the other East. He had\ngone there to accept a hecatomb of sheep and oxen, and was enjoying\nhimself at his festival; but the other gods met in the house of\nOlympian Jove, and the sire of gods and men spoke first. At that moment\nhe was thinking of Aegisthus, who had been killed by Agamemnon’s son\nOrestes; so he said to the other gods:", "“See now, how men lay blame upon us gods for what is after all nothing\nbut their own folly. Look at Aegisthus; he must needs make love to\nAgamemnon’s wife unrighteously and then kill Agamemnon, though he knew\nit would be the death of him; for I sent Mercury to warn him not to do\neither of these things, inasmuch as Orestes would be sure to take his\nrevenge when he grew up and wanted to return home. Mercury told him\nthis in all good will but he would not listen, and now he has paid for\neverything in full.”", "Then Minerva said, “Father, son of Saturn, King of kings, it served\nAegisthus right, and so it would any one else who does as he did; but\nAegisthus is neither here nor there; it is for Ulysses that my heart\nbleeds, when I think of his sufferings in that lonely sea-girt island,\nfar away, poor man, from all his friends. It is an island covered with\nforest, in the very middle of the sea, and a goddess lives there,\ndaughter of the magician Atlas, who looks after the bottom of the\nocean, and carries the great columns that keep heaven and earth\nasunder. This daughter of Atlas has got hold of poor unhappy Ulysses,\nand keeps trying by every kind of blandishment to make him forget his\nhome, so that he is tired of life, and thinks of nothing but how he may\nonce more see the smoke of his own chimneys. You, sir, take no heed of\nthis, and yet when Ulysses was before Troy did he not propitiate you\nwith many a burnt sacrifice? Why then should you keep on being so angry\nwith him?”", "And Jove said, “My child, what are you talking about? How can I forget\nUlysses than whom there is no more capable man on earth, nor more\nliberal in his offerings to the immortal gods that live in heaven? Bear\nin mind, however, that Neptune is still furious with Ulysses for having\nblinded an eye of Polyphemus king of the Cyclopes. Polyphemus is son to\nNeptune by the nymph Thoosa, daughter to the sea-king Phorcys;\ntherefore though he will not kill Ulysses outright, he torments him by\npreventing him from getting home. Still, let us lay our heads together\nand see how we can help him to return; Neptune will then be pacified,\nfor if we are all of a mind he can hardly stand out against us.”"]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Book 1 — The gods in council—Athena’s visit to Ithaca—the challenge from Telemachus to the suitors", "paragraphs": ["Tell me, Muse, of that clever hero who wandered far and wide after he destroyed the famous city of Troy. He visited many cities and learned the customs of many peoples. He suffered greatly at sea while trying to save his own life and bring his men home safely. But no matter what he did, he could not save his companions—they died through their own reckless folly when they ate the cattle of the sun god Helios. So the god prevented them from ever reaching home. Tell me about all these things, daughter of Zeus, from whatever source you know them.", "Now all those who had escaped death in battle or shipwreck had returned safely home—all except Odysseus. Though he longed to return to his wife and homeland, the goddess Calypso held him captive in her vast cave, wanting to marry him. But as the years passed, the time came when the gods decided he should return to Ithaca. Even then, when he was among his own people, his troubles would not be over. Nevertheless, all the gods had begun to pity him except Poseidon, who continued to persecute him relentlessly and would not let him reach home.", "Now Poseidon had gone to the Ethiopians, who live at the world's edge and are divided in two groups, one facing west and the other east. He had gone there to receive an offering of a hundred sheep and oxen, and was enjoying himself at the feast. But the other gods gathered in the palace of Olympian Zeus, and the father of gods and men spoke first. At that moment he was thinking of Aegisthus, who had been killed by Agamemnon's son Orestes. So he said to the other gods:", "\"Look how mortals blame us gods for what is really nothing but their own foolishness. Take Aegisthus—he had to seduce Agamemnon's wife and then murder Agamemnon, even though he knew it would mean his death. I sent Hermes to warn him not to do either of these things, since Orestes would surely take revenge when he grew up and wanted to return home. Hermes gave him this warning with the best intentions, but Aegisthus wouldn't listen. Now he has paid the full price for everything.\"", "Then Athena said, \"Father, son of Cronos, king of kings, Aegisthus got what he deserved, and so would anyone else who acts as he did. But Aegisthus doesn't matter to me—it's Odysseus who breaks my heart when I think of his suffering on that lonely island surrounded by sea, far from all his friends. It's a forested island in the middle of the ocean, and a goddess lives there—the daughter of Atlas the magician, who watches over the ocean depths and holds up the great pillars that separate heaven and earth. This daughter of Atlas has trapped poor unhappy Odysseus and keeps trying with every kind of charm to make him forget his home. He's grown weary of life and thinks of nothing but seeing the smoke rising from his own chimneys once more. Yet you pay no attention to this. Didn't Odysseus honor you with many burnt offerings when he was at Troy? Why do you remain so angry with him?\"", "Zeus replied, \"My child, what are you saying? How could I forget Odysseus? There's no mortal more capable than he, nor more generous in his offerings to the immortal gods who live in heaven. But remember that Poseidon is still furious with Odysseus for blinding the eye of Polyphemus, king of the Cyclopes. Polyphemus is Poseidon's son by the nymph Thoosa, daughter of the sea-king Phorcys. So while Poseidon won't kill Odysseus outright, he torments him by preventing him from reaching home. Still, let's put our heads together and figure out how to help him return. Then Poseidon will be appeased—if we're all in agreement, he can hardly stand against us alone.\""]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Sang 1 — Guderne holder råd — Athenas besøg i Ithaka — Telemachos' udfordring til bejlerne", "paragraphs": ["Fortæl mig, Muse, om den snedige helt, der vandrede vidt omkring efter at have lagt den berømte by Troja i ruiner. Han besøgte mange byer og lærte mange folks skikke at kende. Han led hårdt på havet, mens han kæmpede for at redde sit eget liv og bringe sine mænd sikkert hjem. Men uanset hvad han gjorde, kunne han ikke redde sine ledsagere — de døde af deres egen dumdristige hovmod, da de spiste solguden Helios' kvæg. Derfor forhindrede guden dem i nogensinde at nå hjem. Fortæl mig om alt dette, datter af Zeus, fra hvilken kilde du end kender det.", "Nu var alle de andre, der havde undsluppet døden i kamp eller skibbrud, vendt sikkert hjem — alle undtagen Odysseus. Skønt han længtes efter at vende tilbage til sin hustru og sit hjemland, holdt gudinden Kalypso ham fanget i sin vældige grotte og ville giftes med ham. Men da årene gik, kom tiden, hvor guderne besluttede, at han skulle vende hjem til Ithaka. Selv da, midt blandt sit eget folk, ville hans prøvelser ikke være ovre. Ikke desto mindre havde alle guderne fået medlidenhed med ham — alle undtagen Poseidon, der uforsonligt forfulgte ham og nægtede at lade ham nå hjem.", "Nu var Poseidon rejst til etiopierne, der lever ved verdens yderste rand og er delt i to grupper, én mod vest og én mod øst. Han var rejst derhen for at modtage en offergave på hundrede får og okser og nød festmåltidet. Men de øvrige guder var forsamlet i den olympiske Zeus' palads, og guders og menneskers fader talte først. I det øjeblik tænkte han på Aigisthos, der var blevet dræbt af Agamemnons søn Orestes. Og han sagde til de andre guder:", "\"Se bare, hvordan de dødelige giver os guder skylden for noget, der intet er andet end deres egen tåbelighed. Tag Aigisthos — han måtte forføre Agamemnons hustru og derefter myrde Agamemnon, skønt han vidste, at det ville koste ham livet. Jeg sendte Hermes for at advare ham mod begge dele, for Orestes ville med sikkerhed tage hævn, når han blev voksen og længtes efter sit hjem. Hermes bragte ham denne advarsel i den bedste mening, men Aigisthos ville ikke lytte. Nu har han betalt den fulde pris for det hele.\"", "Da sagde Athena: \"Fader, søn af Kronos, kongernes konge, Aigisthos fik, hvad han fortjente, og det ville enhver anden, der handlede som han. Men Aigisthos betyder intet for mig — det er Odysseus, der knuser mit hjerte, når jeg tænker på hans lidelser på den ensomme ø omgivet af hav, langt fra alle sine venner. Det er en skovklædt ø midt i oceanet, og en gudinde bor der — datter af troldmanden Atlas, der vogter havets dyb og bærer de mægtige søjler, som skiller himmel og jord. Denne datter af Atlas har fanget den stakkels ulykkelige Odysseus og forsøger med al slags fortryllelse at få ham til at glemme sit hjem. Han er træt af livet og tænker kun på at se røgen stige fra sine egne skorstene igen. Og dog skænker du dette ingen opmærksomhed. Ærede Odysseus dig ikke med mange brændofre, da han var ved Troja? Hvorfor forbliver du så vred på ham?\"", "Zeus svarede: \"Mit barn, hvad er det, du siger? Hvordan kunne jeg glemme Odysseus? Der findes ingen dødelig, der er mere dygtig end han, ej heller mere gavmild med sine ofre til de udødelige guder i himlen. Men husk, at Poseidon stadig er rasende på Odysseus for at have blindet Polyfems øje — kyklopernes hersker. Polyfem er Poseidons søn med nymfen Thoosa, datter af havkongen Forkys. Så selvom Poseidon ikke slår Odysseus ihjel, plager han ham ved at forhindre ham i at nå hjem. Lad os imidlertid lægge hovederne sammen og finde ud af, hvordan vi kan hjælpe ham med at vende tilbage. Så vil Poseidon falde til ro — er vi alle enige, kan han næppe stå imod os alene.\""]}}, "cover": "assets/odyssey.jpg", "coverTone": "#203946", "typeset": true}, {"id": "crime-and-punishment", "title": "Crime and Punishment", "author": "Fyodor Dostoevsky", "preface": ["The novel is also the story of his sister Dunya, who is about to marry a man she does not love to save the family from ruin; of his mother, whose love is itself a kind of pressure; of his friend Razumikhin, whose decency anchors him; of Sonya, the gentle prostitute whose life is one long act of self-sacrifice; and of Svidrigailov, the rich predator who is the novel's image of what Raskolnikov could become if his theory were true.", "Written in 1866 in monthly installments under crushing financial pressure, Crime and Punishment is one of the most morally serious novels ever written and one of the most readable. Dostoevsky uses the structure of a thriller — a murder, a manhunt, a confession — to ask a question philosophy was just then beginning to take seriously: if there is no God, is everything permitted? The novel does not give a tidy answer. It gives Raskolnikov's body, refusing the theory his mind has produced."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Raskolnikov"], "subtitle": "Rodya", "body": "A poor and brilliant former student of law, twenty-three, living in a coffin-sized room in St. Petersburg. He has worked out a theory of extraordinary men, kills an old pawnbroker to test it, and then spends the rest of the novel disintegrating. He is proud, generous, cruel, tender, sick, lucid, and contradictory. The whole novel is the inside of his head, and the novel's argument is that the head he has built cannot live with what his hands have done."}, {"aliases": ["Sonya Marmeladov"], "subtitle": "Faith as the Answer", "body": "A nineteen-year-old who has gone on the streets to feed her drunkard father's starving children. She is meek, devout, completely without irony. She listens to Raskolnikov's confession without flinching. She offers to follow him to Siberia. She is the novel's moral pole, and Dostoevsky writes her as an actual person — poor, humiliated, occasionally exasperated — not a saint. Her love, almost stupid by argument, is what eventually pulls Raskolnikov back into the human world."}, {"aliases": ["Porfiry Petrovich"], "subtitle": "The Investigator", "body": "Brilliant, jovial, patient, dangerous. He suspects Raskolnikov from very early on but plays an unsettling, talkative game with him for most of the novel — letting Raskolnikov talk himself toward confession rather than confronting him. He is the novel's image of intelligence in the service of the moral law, and his long conversations with Raskolnikov are some of the finest dialogue Dostoevsky ever wrote."}, {"aliases": ["Dunya"], "subtitle": "Avdotya Romanovna", "body": "Raskolnikov's sister, beautiful and proud. She has agreed to marry the cold and calculating Luzhin to save the family from ruin — a sacrifice her brother cannot bear. Her plotline runs in parallel to his: a different test of moral strength under pressure, a different decision about what one is willing to barter for security. She refuses Luzhin. She survives Svidrigailov. She marries the decent Razumikhin. She is the novel's quiet counterargument to its hero."}, {"aliases": ["Svidrigailov"], "subtitle": "The Mirror", "body": "A rich, sensual, possibly murderous landowner who has set his sights on Dunya. He is what Raskolnikov's theory looks like in a man who never bothered to talk himself into it — pure appetite, pure will, pure indifference to other people. He says aloud the things Raskolnikov has only thought, and his presence is the novel's image of the future Raskolnikov is fighting against. His final scene is one of the most haunting in the book."}, {"aliases": ["Razumikhin"], "subtitle": "The Friend", "body": "A bear of a man, perpetually broke, perpetually cheerful, devoted to Raskolnikov despite everything. He nurses Raskolnikov through fever, manages his affairs, falls in love with Dunya. He is Dostoevsky's portrait of ordinary decency — not brilliant, not theoretical, simply present. The novel needs him: against the spiraling consciousness of its hero, Razumikhin is what an undamaged human being looks like. He is the floor everyone else is standing on."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Part 1, Chapter 1", "paragraphs": ["On an exceptionally hot evening early in July a young man came out of the garret in which he lodged in S. Place and walked slowly, as though in hesitation, towards K. bridge.", "He had successfully avoided meeting his landlady on the staircase. His garret was under the roof of a high, five-storied house and was more like a cupboard than a room. The landlady who provided him with garret, dinners, and attendance, lived on the floor below, and every time he went out he was obliged to pass her kitchen, the door of which invariably stood open. And each time he passed, the young man had a sick, frightened feeling, which made him scowl and feel ashamed. He was hopelessly in debt to his landlady, and was afraid of meeting her.", "This was not because he was cowardly and abject, quite the contrary; but for some time past he had been in an overstrained irritable condition, verging on hypochondria. He had become so completely absorbed in himself, and isolated from his fellows that he dreaded meeting, not only his landlady, but anyone at all. He was crushed by poverty, but the anxieties of his position had of late ceased to weigh upon him. He had given up attending to matters of practical importance; he had lost all desire to do so. Nothing that any landlady could do had a real terror for him. But to be stopped on the stairs, to be forced to listen to her trivial, irrelevant gossip, to pestering demands for payment, threats and complaints, and to rack his brains for excuses, to prevaricate, to lie--no, rather than that, he would creep down the stairs like a cat and slip out unseen.", "This evening, however, on coming out into the street, he became acutely aware of his fears.", "“I want to attempt a thing _like that_ and am frightened by these trifles,” he thought, with an odd smile. “Hm... yes, all is in a man’s hands and he lets it all slip from cowardice, that’s an axiom. It would be interesting to know what it is men are most afraid of. Taking a new step, uttering a new word is what they fear most.... But I am talking too much. It’s because I chatter that I do nothing. Or perhaps it is that I chatter because I do nothing. I’ve learned to chatter this last month, lying for days together in my den thinking... of Jack the Giant-killer. Why am I going there now? Am I capable of _that_? Is _that_ serious? It is not serious at all. It’s simply a fantasy to amuse myself; a plaything! Yes, maybe it is a plaything.”", "The heat in the street was terrible: and the airlessness, the bustle and the plaster, scaffolding, bricks, and dust all about him, and that special Petersburg stench, so familiar to all who are unable to get out of town in summer--all worked painfully upon the young man’s already overwrought nerves. The insufferable stench from the pot-houses, which are particularly numerous in that part of the town, and the drunken men whom he met continually, although it was a working day, completed the revolting misery of the picture. An expression of the profoundest disgust gleamed for a moment in the young man’s refined face. He was, by the way, exceptionally handsome, above the average in height, slim, well-built, with beautiful dark eyes and dark brown hair. Soon he sank into deep thought, or more accurately speaking into a complete blankness of mind; he walked along not observing what was about him and not caring to observe it. From time to time, he would mutter something, from the habit of talking to himself, to which he had just confessed. At these moments he would become conscious that his ideas were sometimes in a tangle and that he was very weak; for two days he had scarcely tasted food."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Part 1, Chapter 1", "paragraphs": ["On an unbearably hot evening in early July, a young man stepped out of the tiny room he rented on S. Place and walked slowly, almost hesitantly, toward K. Bridge.", "He had managed to avoid his landlady on the staircase. His room was tucked under the roof of a tall five-story building and was more like a closet than a proper room. The landlady, who provided him with the room, meals, and basic service, lived on the floor below. Every time he went out, he had to pass her kitchen, and the door was always open. Each time he walked by, the young man felt a wave of sickness and dread that made him grimace with shame. He was hopelessly in debt to her and terrified of running into her.", "This was not because he was a coward -- far from it. But for some time now he had been in an agitated, irritable state bordering on nervous collapse. He had become so completely wrapped up in himself, so cut off from everyone, that he dreaded encountering not just his landlady but anyone at all. He was crushed by poverty, but lately even that had stopped weighing on him. He had given up dealing with practical matters entirely and had lost all desire to try. Nothing his landlady could actually do truly frightened him. But to be stopped on the stairs, forced to listen to her pointless gossip, her nagging demands for payment, her threats and complaints, to have to scramble for excuses, to dodge and lie -- no, he would rather creep down the stairs like a cat and slip out unseen.", "This evening, however, stepping out into the street, he became sharply aware of his fear.", "\"I want to attempt a thing _like that_ and I'm scared of these little things,\" he thought with a strange smile. \"Hm... yes, everything is in a man's hands, and he lets it all slip away out of cowardice -- that's a given. It would be interesting to know what people are most afraid of. Taking a new step, saying a new word -- that's what terrifies them most.... But I talk too much. I talk because I do nothing. Or maybe I do nothing because I talk. I've learned to talk this past month, lying around for days in my hole thinking... about fairy tales. Why am I going there now? Am I capable of _that_? Is _that_ serious? No, it's not serious at all. It's just a fantasy to entertain myself -- a game! Yes, maybe it's just a game.\"", "The heat on the street was brutal -- the stagnant air, the crowds, the plaster, scaffolding, bricks, and dust everywhere, and that distinctive Petersburg stench so familiar to anyone who can't get out of the city in summer -- it all scraped painfully against the young man's already frayed nerves. The unbearable reek from the bars, which were especially numerous in this part of town, and the drunks he kept running into despite it being a workday, completed the picture of misery. A look of profound disgust flickered across the young man's fine features. He was, as it happened, exceptionally handsome -- taller than average, slim, well-built, with striking dark eyes and dark brown hair. Soon he sank into deep thought, or more accurately into a kind of mental blankness. He walked along without noticing his surroundings and without caring to. From time to time he would mutter something under his breath -- a habit of talking to himself that he had just acknowledged. In these moments he would realize that his thoughts were tangled and that he was very weak. He had barely eaten in two days."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Første del, kapitel 1", "paragraphs": ["En ulidelig varm aften i begyndelsen af juli trådte en ung mand ud af det bitte lille værelse, han lejede på S. Plads, og gik langsomt, næsten tøvende, i retning af K. Bro.", "Det var lykkedes ham at undgå sin værtinde på trappen. Hans værelse lå under taget i en høj femeetagers bygning og mindede mere om et skab end et rigtigt værelse. Værtinden, som stillede værelset, måltider og grundlæggende service til rådighed, boede på etagen nedenunder. Hver gang han gik ud, måtte han forbi hendes køkken, og døren stod altid åben. Hver gang han passerede, følte den unge mand en bølge af kvalme og angst, der fik ham til at grimassere af skam. Han var håbløst forgældet til hende og skræklagen for at støde ind i hende.", "Det var ikke fordi han var en kujon -- langtfra. Men i nogen tid havde han befundet sig i en ophidset, irritabel tilstand på grænsen til nervesammenbrud. Han var blevet så fuldstændig opslugt af sig selv, så afskåret fra alle, at han frygtede at møde ikke bare sin værtinde, men hvem som helst overhovedet. Han var knust af fattigdom, men på det seneste var selv det holdt op med at tynge ham. Han havde opgivet at tage sig af praktiske anliggender helt og havde mistet enhver lyst til at prøve. Intet hans værtinde reelt kunne gøre, skræmte ham. Men at blive standset på trappen, tvunget til at lytte til hendes meningsløse sladder, hendes evige krav om betaling, hendes trusler og klager -- at skulle finde på undskyldninger, undvige og lyve -- nej, så ville han hellere liste ned ad trappen som en kat og smutte ud usét.", "Men denne aften, da han trådte ud på gaden, blev han skarpt bevidst om sin frygt.", "»Jeg vil forsøge en ting som _den_, og så er jeg bange for den slags småting,« tænkte han med et underligt smil. »Hm... ja, alt ligger i menneskets egne hænder, og alligevel lader han det hele glide sig af hænde af ren fejhed -- det er en kendsgerning. Det kunne være interessant at vide, hvad folk frygter mest. At tage et nyt skridt, sige et nyt ord -- det er det, der skræmmer dem mest... Men jeg taler for meget. Jeg taler, fordi jeg intet foretager mig. Eller måske foretager jeg mig intet, fordi jeg taler. Det har jeg lært at gøre denne sidste måned, mens jeg har ligget i mit hul og tænkt over... eventyr. Hvorfor går jeg derhen nu? Er jeg i stand til _det_? Er _det_ alvorligt? Nej, det er slet ikke alvorligt. Det er bare et fantasifoster til at underholde mig selv -- et spil! Ja, måske er det bare et spil.«", "Heden på gaden var brutal -- den stillestående luft, trængslen, pudsen, stilladser, mursten og støv overalt, og den karakteristiske petersborgske stank, så velkendt for alle, der ikke kan komme ud af byen om sommeren -- alt sammen skrabede smertefuldt mod den unge mands i forvejen flossede nerver. Den uudholdelige stank fra barerne, som var særligt talrige i denne del af byen, og de berusede, han hele tiden stødte ind i trods det var en hverdag, fuldstændiggjorde elendighedens billede. Et glimt af dyb afsky flakkede hen over den unge mands fine træk. Han var, som det tilfældigvis forholdt sig, usædvanlig flot -- højere end gennemsnittet, slank, velbygget, med slående mørke øjne og mørkebrunt hår. Snart sank han ned i dyb tanke, eller mere præcist ind i en slags mental tomhed. Han gik videre uden at lægge mærke til sine omgivelser og uden at bekymre sig om det. Ind imellem mumlede han noget for sig selv -- en vane med at tale med sig selv, som han netop havde erkendt. I de øjeblikke blev han klar over, at hans tanker var filtrede, og at han var meget svag. Han havde næsten ikke spist i to dage."]}}, "cover": "assets/crime-and-punishment.jpg", "coverTone": "#493c2e", "typeset": true}, {"id": "pride-and-prejudice", "title": "Pride and Prejudice", "author": "Jane Austen", "preface": ["Around this central plot, Austen weaves four others: the marriages (or near-marriages) of Elizabeth's four sisters, the social fortunes of the absurd Mr. Collins, and the long shadow of Wickham — the charming officer whose lies Elizabeth believes early and discovers late. The Bennet household, presided over by an indifferent father and a frantic mother, supplies the comedy. The world beyond the household — the entail, the small income, the necessity of marriage as a financial fact for women — supplies the stakes.", "Published in 1813, after fifteen years of revision (Austen began it in 1796 as \"First Impressions\"), Pride and Prejudice is the most beloved novel in English. The opening sentence is the most famous opening in fiction. The dialogue invented free indirect discourse a century before modernism formally claimed it. The plot is romantic comedy in the strict technical sense — courtship, obstacles, recognition, marriage — but the moral architecture beneath is severe: about how easily first impressions are mistaken for judgment, about the gap between the people one appears to be and the person one is, about the work it takes to see another person clearly enough to love them well."], "characters": [{"aliases": ["Elizabeth Bennet"], "subtitle": "Lizzy", "body": "The second of five sisters, the sharpest mind in the family, her father's favorite. She is twenty, witty, proud of her own discernment, and — she discovers — wrong about almost everything that matters. The novel is the slow correction of her judgment, and the slow recognition that the man she most despised is the man she has most undervalued. She becomes, in the course of the book, the most clear-eyed character in English fiction."}, {"aliases": ["Mr. Darcy"], "subtitle": "Master of Pemberley", "body": "Wealthy, proud, awkward, and — beneath all that — deeply principled. He insults Elizabeth at their first meeting and spends the rest of the novel correcting the version of himself that produced the insult. His first proposal is one of the worst proposals in literature. His second is one of the most earned. The progress between them is the moral architecture of the book."}, {"aliases": ["Jane Bennet"], "subtitle": "The Eldest Sister", "body": "Beautiful, gentle, slow to judge anyone harshly. She falls in love with Mr. Bingley, Darcy's friend, at the same ball where Elizabeth meets Darcy. Their romance is the novel's quiet B-plot — straightforward where Elizabeth and Darcy's is complicated. Jane's near-loss of Bingley shows what is at stake for women in this world: the wrong rumor, the wrong friend, the wrong moment, and the marriage that would have made you safe is gone."}, {"aliases": ["Mr. Bennet"], "subtitle": "The Father", "body": "Master of Longbourn, an estate that will pass to a male cousin on his death. He is intelligent, witty, and emotionally absent — preferring his library to his family. The novel is gently severe about him: his irony is funny, but his unwillingness to engage with his daughters' future is a moral failure. He loves Elizabeth without doing anything to protect her."}, {"aliases": ["Mrs. Bennet"], "subtitle": "The Mother", "body": "Frantic, undignified, single-minded about getting her daughters married — and, in the world of the novel, completely correct that this is the only thing that matters. Austen makes her ridiculous, and then keeps reminding you that her ridiculousness is the rational response to a system in which her daughters' survival depends on five marriages. The book's most uncomfortable balance: she is laughed at, and she is right."}, {"aliases": ["Mr. Wickham"], "subtitle": "The Charming Officer", "body": "A handsome militia officer, full of anecdotes about his hard usage at the hands of Mr. Darcy — most of them lies. Elizabeth believes him, and the novel spends much of its middle section unpicking why she did. He elopes with the youngest Bennet sister Lydia, threatening the family's whole respectability, and is bought off by Darcy. He is the novel's most polished portrait of a charming man you should not trust."}], "excerpts": {"original-en": {"title": "Chapter 1", "paragraphs": ["It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune must be in want of a wife.", "However little known the feelings or views of such a man may be on his first entering a neighbourhood, this truth is so well fixed in the minds of the surrounding families, that he is considered as the rightful property of some one or other of their daughters.", "“My dear Mr. Bennet,” said his lady to him one day, “have you heard that Netherfield Park is let at last?”", "Mr. Bennet replied that he had not.", "“But it is,” returned she; “for Mrs. Long has just been here, and she told me all about it.”", "Mr. Bennet made no answer."]}, "modern-en": {"title": "Chapter 1", "paragraphs": ["It is a truth universally acknowledged that a single man with a large fortune must be looking for a wife.", "No matter how little is known about such a man's feelings or intentions when he first moves into a neighborhood, this truth is so firmly established in the minds of the surrounding families that he is considered the rightful property of one or another of their daughters.", "\"My dear Mr. Bennet,\" his wife said to him one day, \"have you heard that Netherfield Park has finally been rented?\"", "Mr. Bennet replied that he had not.", "\"But it has,\" she returned. \"Mrs. Long has just been here, and she told me all about it.\"", "Mr. Bennet made no answer."]}, "modern-da": {"title": "Kapitel 1", "paragraphs": ["Det er en alment anerkendt sandhed, at en ugift mand med en stor formue nødvendigvis må være på udkig efter en kone.", "Uanset hvor lidt man ved om en sådan mands følelser eller hensigter, når han først flytter ind i et nabolag, er denne sandhed så dybt forankret i de omkringboende familiers bevidsthed, at han betragtes som den retmæssige ejendom for den ene eller den anden af deres døtre.", "»Min kære Mr. Bennet,« sagde hans kone til ham en dag, »har du hørt, at Netherfield Park endelig er blevet lejet ud?«", "Mr. Bennet svarede, at det havde han ikke.", "»Men det er det,« svarede hun. »Mrs. Long har lige været her, og hun fortalte mig alt om det.«", "Mr. Bennet svarede ikke."]}}, "cover": "assets/pride-and-prejudice.jpg", "coverTone": "#354738", "typeset": true}];
